"Carson, 76, is celebrated for works that often cross boundaries between poetry, fiction and scholarship.", "body_original": "'Bold and inventive' Canadian poet Anne Carson wins Nobel Literature Prize\nBy\nIan Youngs\nCulture reporter\nPublished\n8 October 2026\nCanadian poet, essayist and translator Anne Carson has been awarded this year's Nobel Prize in Literature.\nCarson, 76, is celebrated for works that often cross the boundaries between poetry, fiction and scholarship.\nThe Nobel Prize committee said they had selected her \"for her bold and inventive oeuvre that, in playful dialogue with the classical tradition, has created new forms for contemporary literature\".\nCarson responded by joking that the judges were \"crazy\" for honouring her. \"I think they're all crazy... Well, you know, it's crazy,\" she told public broadcaster RUV in Iceland, where she is currently staying.\nThe Nobel Prize is awarded for a lifetime's body of work and is regarded as the most prestigious award in global literature, with the winner receiving 12 million Swedish kronor (£900,000, $1.2m).\nIngrid Carlberg, permanent secretary of the Swedish Academy, said she had broken the news to Carson on the phone shortly before the public announcement.\n\"She had time to tell me was that she is, for the moment, in Iceland, where she is listening to the wind,\" Carlberg, said.\n\"She was taken by surprise but very happy. I could hear that in her voice.\"\nBorn in Toronto in 1950, Carson lives in Michigan and has also held Icelandic citizenship since 2022.\nAnders Olsson, chairman of the Swedish Academy's Nobel committee for Literature, praised the way Carson has \"broken away from lyrical poetry bound to the narrative self and inspired new forms of contemporary literature\".\n\"This method of writing leads to playful and ironic works that move not only between classical and modern poetry but also between different genres and artforms,\" he said.\nCanadian Prime Minister Mark Carney said Carson's win was \"an extraordinary achievement and a proud moment for Canada\".\nThe writer's works feature \"wit, imagination, and a style entirely her own,\" Carney wrote on social media.\n\"She has brought ancient stories to new audiences and transformed the way we think about literature.\"\n'Outstanding importance'\nCarson's notable works include The Glass Essay, Autobiography of Red: A Novel in Verse, Nox, Float and The Beauty of the Husband: A Fictional Essay in 29 Tangos.\nIn 2019, her performance piece Norma Jeane Baker of Troy drew a line between famous beauties from different historical periouds - Helen of Troy and Marilyn Monroe.\nIn 2021, she released The Trojan Women: A Comic, a graphic retelling of Euripides' ancient story; and her translation of Elektra by Sophocles was staged starring Brie Larson in London last year.\nShe has been a professor of classics for more than 30 years and her writing is often inspired by Greek literature.\nHer translations have often given a contemporary twist to ancient works.\n\"Her collaborations with visual artists and choreographers and with opera and theatre directors demonstrate the freedom with which she relates to literary expectations and rule systems,\" Olsson said.\n\"It is this that lies behind the outstanding importance her work since the 1990s has had for the international art and literature scene.\"\nRelated topics\nBooks\nLiterature\nNobel Prize\nMore on this story\nHungarian author László Krasznahorkai wins Nobel Literature Prize\nPublished\n9 October 2025\nSouth Korea's Han Kang wins Nobel Literature Prize\nPublished\n10 October 2024\nNorway's Jon Fosse wins Nobel Literature Prize\nPublished\n5 October 2023", "fetched_at": "2026-10-11T00:23:16.183990", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "ノーベル", "s": 0.125, "e": 0.613, "sm": 125, "em": 612, "cs": 0, "ce": 4, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "賞", "s": 0.625, "e": 0.875, "sm": 625, "em": 875, "cs": 4, "ce": 5, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "の", "s": 0.887, "e": 0.987, "sm": 887, "em": 98